spheres.full_exact_source_text:
# Sphere of Sect Stewardship
<small><em>Qi Expression: Primary — Mandate-Order Qi; Supporting — Earth Qi, Law Qi, Resource Qi, Human-Relation Qi.</em></small>
“A sect is not only mountains, halls, scriptures, and elders. A sect is the ledger of who fed whom, who carried whose message, who cleaned the furnace before dawn, and who remembered the name of the lowest servant when Heaven asked for witnesses.”
Common Name: Sect Life Sphere
Formal Name: Sect Stewardship Sphere
Sphere Type: Non-Combat Civic Sphere
Primary Users: mortal farmers, outer disciples, sect servants, clerks, stewards, hall apprentices, mission runners, quartermasters, instructors, deacons, elders, branch founders, and sect administrators
Primary Path: Any. This sphere is not inherently a combat art and does not require supernatural cultivation at Mortal access.
Primary
Resource: Merit Dice, institutional Standing, time, favors, access, records, and duties
Secondary
Resource: CP, Stamina, or Resonance only when a talent explicitly turns sect obligation into a supernatural cultivation technique
Common Effects: sect duties, contribution ledgers, mission boards, stipend access, Scripture Pavilion permissions, dormitory networks, senior and junior relationships, hall assignments, public lectures, sect tokens, resource petitions, branch halls, archives, disciple registers, steward authority, non-combat logistics, sect reputation, apprenticeship, governance, and strategic projects
Player-Facing Note: This sphere is for characters who want sect life, halls, duties, contribution ledgers, mission access, public lectures, archives, logistics, and institutional reputation to matter at the table. It is not a combat sphere and does not improve attacks, damage, save DCs, or initiative.
### Dao Check
Sect Stewardship cultivates belonging as obligation.
The sphere is not about commanding plants, refining pills, striking enemies, or winning duels. It is the Dao of being placed within a living institution and learning how service becomes permission, permission becomes access, access becomes responsibility, and responsibility becomes authority.
The central question is:
What do you owe, who recognizes it, and what door opens because the ledger remembers you?
The recurring verb is Record.
Sect Stewardship should always answer through ledgers, duties, names, tokens, seniority, missions, stipends, courtyards, lectures, halls, and oaths. If a talent would work just as well for a lone hermit with no witnesses, no obligations, no social structure, and no records, it probably belongs in another sphere.
The core loop is:
Take a Place. Know your role in the sect, clan, village, hall, or household.
Accept a Duty. Choose a useful obligation that gives your presence meaning.
Record the Merit. Convert service into recognized credit, reputation, or permission.
Petition the Hall. Spend that recognition on access, supplies, instruction, protection, or help.
Return the Debt. The benefit creates a future duty, relationship, expectation, or political consequence.
### Sphere Boundary
Sect Stewardship governs organized sect life, contribution systems, institutional reputation, non-combat missions, clerical authority, resource access, public lectures, teaching registers, dormitory networks, archives, halls, stipends, and branch administration.
Sect Stewardship does not govern:
creating food, herbs, minerals, or resources from nothing;
magically growing plants;
harvesting spiritual materials from the wild;
refining pills, elixirs, or formulae;
crafting talismans, arrays, weapons, or artifacts;
commanding NPCs as mind control;
replacing money, rank, lineage, or political authority;
bypassing sect law, story locks, or GM-controlled permissions;
gaining combat bonuses to attack rolls, damage, save DCs, or initiative;
forcing elders, ancestors, rivals, nobles, or other sects to obey you.
The Sphere of Harvesting and Gathering decides how materials are found and taken. The Sphere of Alchemy decides what ingredients become once they reach the cauldron. The future Wood or Growth sphere decides how living plants grow, heal, bloom, or obey. Sect Stewardship decides who is allowed into the herb garden, who signs the mission writ, who records the tithe, who eats in the outer kitchen, and who may request a furnace slot.
### Fiction Baseline
Cultivation sect stories often use layered hierarchies such as servant, outer disciple, inner disciple, core disciple, deacon, elder, sect master, and ancestor. Advancement often depends on cultivation, talent, merit, exams, service, or sponsorship rather than raw level alone.
Common sect-life structures include:
outer gates and inner gates;
dormitories and courtyards;
mission halls and task boards;
contribution points or merit ledgers;
Scripture Pavilions and technique exchange;
treasure halls and medicine halls;
public Dao lectures;
monthly stipends;
servant work, field tending, patrols, errands, and hall assignments;
senior brother and senior sister etiquette;
deacons who manage internal affairs;
elders who teach, judge, protect, or exploit;
rank, face, patronage, and consequences.
Tianxia should use these structures without treating them as one fixed universal ladder. Some sects are orthodox academies. Some are clans. Some are temples. Some are merchant associations. Some are brutal demonic mountain gates. This sphere provides the mechanical language for living inside any of them.
### Non-Combat Safeguard
Sect Stewardship is a non-combat sphere.
Unless a talent explicitly says otherwise, a Sect Stewardship feature cannot:
deal damage;
make an attack;
impose a combat condition;
increase attack rolls, damage rolls, armor class, save DCs, or weapon properties;
summon combat allies;
create cover, difficult terrain, forced movement, or battlefield hazards;
override initiative, action economy, or hostile creatures in combat.
Some talents may prevent a fight, delay a challenge, arrange arbitration, secure escorts, or obtain protection during downtime. Those are social and logistical effects, not combat actions.
A GM may suspend Merit spending in a scene where no institution, witness, token, social relationship, or record could plausibly matter.
## Core Sect Stewardship Rules
### Learning Sect Stewardship
When you learn Sect Stewardship, choose Intelligence, Wisdom, or Charisma as your Sect Stewardship key ability.
Choose Intelligence if your stewardship is based on records, law, administration, scheduling, scripture catalogues, and logistics.
Choose Wisdom if your stewardship is based on reading people, judging duties, teaching juniors, noticing waste, and understanding sect customs.
Choose Charisma if your stewardship is based on face, introductions, mediation, patronage, public speech, and social leverage.
You gain proficiency in one of the following skills or tools:
History;
Insight;
Investigation;
Persuasion;
Religion;
Calligrapher’s Supplies;
Cook’s Utensils;
Herbalism Kit;
a tool appropriate to your sect duty, with GM approval.
If you already have proficiency in the chosen skill or tool, gain a flat +2 bonus to checks with it when the check directly concerns sect life, records, duties, ledgers, halls, missions, etiquette, or institutional logistics.
### Sect Check
When a Sect Stewardship talent calls for a Sect Check, roll:
d20 + Proficiency Bonus + Sect Stewardship key ability modifier
You may spend one Merit Die before or after rolling the d20 but before the GM declares the result. Roll the Merit Die and add it to the check.
Unless a talent says otherwise, you cannot add more than one Merit Die to the same d20 roll.
### Institutions
Sect Stewardship works inside an institution.
An institution can be a cultivation sect, clan, academy, temple, village cooperative, merchant hall, guard office, noble estate, medicine hall, library, pilgrim lodge, monastic order, or comparable organized body.
When the sphere says “sect,” read it as “the institution you are currently serving,” unless the talent depends on a true cultivation sect.
The institution must have at least some of the following:
recognized roles;
duties;
records;
stores;
hierarchy;
tokens, passes, signs, uniforms, or seals;
people who care whether obligations are kept.
A lone wilderness camp, a random crowd, or a band of strangers usually does not count until you create an organized agreement with them.
### Standing
Standing measures how an institution currently recognizes you.
Standing is not cultivation Realm, social class, noble rank, or universal reputation. It is local and contextual.
There are five ordinary Standing grades.
No Standing. You are a stranger, guest, prisoner, petitioner, or unknown worker.
Servant Standing. You may perform basic labor, receive food or shelter, and be ordered around.
Outer Standing. You are trusted for ordinary duties, simple missions, shared facilities, and basic records.
Inner Standing. You are trusted with restricted duties, better instruction, inner facilities, low-grade resources, and sensitive errands.
Core Standing. You are trusted with disciples, stores, restricted archives, important missions, hall authority, or a named courtyard.
Some institutions may also recognize Elder Standing, Guest Elder Standing, Lineage Heir Standing, Clan Standing, Official Standing, or other campaign-specific statuses. These are story positions and are not automatically gained from this sphere.
When you learn Sect Stewardship, choose one institution where you have Servant or Outer Standing, subject to GM approval. If the campaign begins before you have joined any group, you instead gain the Petitioner status and can use the sphere to earn your first recognized place.
### Merit Dice
Sect Stewardship uses Merit Dice.
Your Merit Die begins as a d4.
Your maximum number of Merit Dice is equal to your Proficiency Bonus, minimum 1.
You regain all spent Merit Dice when you finish a Long Rest if you are in good standing with at least one institution and spend at least 10 minutes fulfilling a duty, checking a ledger, reporting to a superior, instructing a junior, cleaning a shared space, or otherwise honoring your role.
If you are away from any institution, you regain one spent Merit Die when you finish a Long Rest by maintaining records, writing reports, preserving tokens, planning duties, or honoring the sect’s rules while traveling.
You can spend Merit Dice only on Sect Stewardship features unless another rule says otherwise.
Merit Dice cannot be converted into gold, trade goods, CXP, pills, talents, items, or political office unless a talent explicitly permits a limited exchange.
### Sect Points and Contribution Ledgers
Some campaigns use Sect Points, abbreviated SP, for contribution credit.
Sect Points are not the same as Merit Dice.
Merit Dice are your personal sphere resource.
Sect Points are institutional currency recorded by a sect, clan, hall, or comparable organization.
Standing controls what you are allowed to request.
Sect Points help pay for approved requests once permission exists.
For ordinary player accounting, 1 SP has about 1 gp of purchasing value inside the sect. Cash exchange is worse: donating 2 gp usually grants 1 SP, and cashing out 2 SP usually grants 1 gp. Rare goods, restricted manuals, special pills, private teachers, secret realm slots, and high-grade materials also require Standing or permission.
Sect Stewardship does not create Sect Points from nothing. It helps you earn, protect, audit, justify, petition with, and spend recognized contribution. A Duty, mission, donation, service, tournament result, or sect office must actually exist before SP is awarded.
If you use the Orthodox Sect Player Primer, use that document’s Sect Point prices, stipends, and rewards as the default economy.
### Taking a Place
When you finish a Long Rest, choose one Sect Role that reflects your current duty.
A role is not a class, job, or permanent rank. It is what you are currently known to be doing.
Common roles include:
Field Hand;
Kitchen Worker;
Messenger;
Dormitory Senior;
Archive Runner;
Medicine Hall Assistant;
Mission Board Reader;
Patrol Clerk;
Guest Attendant;
Stable Hand;
Furnace Cleaner;
Spirit Field Steward;
Lecture Scribe;
Dispute Witness;
Storehouse Aide;
Outer Gate Watcher;
Junior Instructor.
When a non-combat ability check directly relates to your chosen role, you may spend one Merit Die and add it to the check.
Examples:
A Field Hand adds Merit to notice spoiled seed, judge irrigation schedules, or organize harvest labor.
A Kitchen Worker adds Merit to feed a dormitory, identify ration theft, or use the kitchen gossip network.
A Lecture Scribe adds Merit to remember an elder’s teaching or copy a clean set of notes.
A Storehouse Aide adds Merit to find a legal substitute supply or detect forged requisitions.
### Accepting a Duty
Once per Long Rest, you may declare a Duty.
A Duty is a clear non-combat obligation such as:
tend the lower herb terraces;
copy the new outer gate roster;
carry a sealed letter to the west courtyard;
arrange food for visiting disciples;
inspect the dormitory spirit lamps;
find why medicine stores are short;
escort a group of new outer disciples to orientation;
prepare the Mission Hall notices;
help a farmer settle a sect tithe dispute;
keep records for a gathering expedition.
While pursuing your Duty, you may spend Merit Dice on any non-combat ability check that directly advances it, even if the check is not tied to your chosen Sect Role.
When you complete the Duty, choose one reward with GM approval:
regain one spent Merit Die;
improve your Standing for one scene;
gain a minor favor from someone involved;
learn one useful rumor;
secure food, shelter, or basic supplies for yourself and up to five companions;
gain Advantage on one later Sect Check related to the same hall, person, or problem.
Completing a Duty should create a record, witness, favor, or obligation. The GM may deny the reward if no one knows, cares, or can verify that the duty was performed.
### Petitioning the Hall
As a non-combat Action during a scene, or during downtime, you may spend one Merit Die to petition an appropriate hall, superior, clerk, family, steward, or local authority for ordinary support.
Roll the Merit Die.
On a 1-2, you receive only the minimum help that your Standing demands.
On a 3-4, you receive useful help without extra trouble.
On a 5 or higher, if your Merit Die is large enough to roll that result, the support is unusually timely, discreet, generous, or well-matched.
Common petitions include:
a meal and safe sleeping space;
a clean set of robes or work clothes;
mundane tools for a duty;
permission to enter a public hall;
a basic map of the sect grounds;
an introduction to a clerk, senior, farmer, guard, or apprentice;
access to public notices;
use of a common room, kitchen, stable, outer library desk, or messenger channel;
temporary storage for ordinary goods;
a written receipt or witness statement.
Petitioning cannot obtain rare treasures, secret manuals, combat allies, high-ranking interventions, expensive materials, forbidden access, or anything the institution would not plausibly grant.
## Sect Stewardship Talent Tags
Ledger Talents. Improve Merit Dice, records, contribution systems, audits, and bureaucratic reliability.
Duty Talents. Improve sect chores, assigned roles, daily work, service, and practical labor.
Mission Talents. Improve mission boards, risk assessment, contracts, travel preparations, and task completion.
Pavilion Talents. Improve access to scriptures, lectures, archives, libraries, and recorded teaching.
Household Talents. Improve food, lodging, dormitories, kitchens, courtyards, uniforms, tools, and ordinary shared life.
Etiquette Talents. Improve seniority, introductions, mediation, face, apology, rank recognition, and social navigation.
Steward Talents. Improve stores, halls, logistics, supplies, projects, clerks, apprentices, and departmental management.
Lineage Talents. Improve discipleship, patronage, teaching chains, oaths, branch halls, and sect identity.
Realm Evolutions. Open supernatural institutional authority, hall-scale projects, branch founding, and ancestral mandates.
## Mortal Realm Talents
### Outer Gate Routine - Duty, Ledger
You know how to turn repetitive work into recognized merit.
When you complete a Duty that involves ordinary labor, service, cleaning, farming, copying, cooking, carrying, sorting, or tending, regain one spent Merit Die.
You can regain a Merit Die this way once per Long Rest.
In addition, when you spend at least 1 hour helping with ordinary sect chores during downtime, you can secure food and safe lodging for yourself and up to five companions of equal or lower Standing for the next 24 hours.
This does not create food. It obtains a proper share from existing stores, kitchens, dormitories, hostels, or village households.
### Sect Token Etiquette - Etiquette
You know how to read tokens, robes, seals, belts, hall badges, speech patterns, and seniority markers.
You have Advantage on Sect Checks made to identify a person’s apparent Standing, hall affiliation, disciple grade, service role, or whether a displayed sect token looks false.
When you enter a new sect, clan, academy, or organized village, you can spend one Merit Die to avoid a minor etiquette mistake that would otherwise offend a local functionary, guard, outer disciple, or servant.
This does not protect you from deliberate insults, major protocol breaches, or lying about your rank.
### Communal Rice Bowl - Household
You know where ordinary survival lives: kitchens, wells, firewood stores, robe laundries, barns, and servant paths.
When you Petition the Hall for food, lodging, work clothes, common tools, a bath, or safe storage for ordinary possessions, treat a Merit Die result of 1 as a 3.
If an ally lacks Standing but travels under your responsibility, you may include that ally in your petition if the request is ordinary and non-sensitive.
### Dormitory Network - Household, Etiquette
You can use shared sleeping quarters, laundry yards, kitchens, queues, and chore rotations as a rumor network.
After spending at least 10 minutes in a dormitory, kitchen, outer yard, servant hall, or similar place, make a Sect Check.
On a success, learn one useful mundane rumor about one of the following:
a current mission;
a missing person;
a common grievance;
a disliked senior;
a useful clerk;
an upcoming inspection;
a public lecture;
an ordinary resource shortage;
an accepted local custom.
The DC is usually 12 for ordinary gossip, 15 for sensitive hall gossip, or 18 for information people are afraid to repeat.
Spending one Merit Die on the check also lets you avoid revealing that you were asking.
### Notice Board Reader - Mission, Ledger
You read mission boards like weather signs.
When you inspect a task board, public notice, tithe list, duty roster, or mission writ for at least 1 minute, make a Sect Check.
On a success, ask the GM two questions:
Which task is safer than it looks?
Which task is more dangerous than it looks?
Which task is underpaid?
Which task is politically sensitive?
Which task has a hidden sponsor?
Which task matches my current Role or Duty?
Which task would improve my Standing fastest?
Which task is likely to require a sphere or talent we lack?
On a success by 5 or more, ask one additional question.
This talent does not reveal secret traps automatically, but it gives the GM a clear way to signal obvious institutional risk.
### Clean Ledger Hand - Ledger
You are difficult to cheat with paperwork.
You have Advantage on checks to notice forged receipts, altered contribution totals, missing supply entries, false attendance marks, or dishonest duty records.
When you complete a Duty and someone attempts to deny that it happened, you may spend one Merit Die to produce or identify a witness, mark, receipt, schedule entry, or other ordinary proof, if such proof could plausibly exist.
This does not fabricate documents. It means you kept them properly.
### Senior Brother’s Errand - Mission, Etiquette
You know how to carry small responsibilities without inflating them into disasters.
When you Accept a Duty that involves carrying a message, token, package, guest, junior disciple, or instruction from one place to another, gain one additional Merit Die that can be spent only on checks made to complete that Duty.
This temporary Merit Die vanishes when the Duty is completed or abandoned.
If the item is sealed, sacred, dangerous, illegal, or politically explosive, this talent does not make it safe. It only helps you handle the errand correctly.
### Common Scripture Notes - Pavilion
You know how to take practical notes from public instruction.
After attending a public lesson, outer gate lecture, village teaching, senior disciple demonstration, or basic scripture reading for at least 1 hour, choose one skill, tool, or sphere topic discussed in the lesson.
Before your next Long Rest, you or one ally who studies your notes may add one Merit Die to one non-combat ability check related to that topic.
This cannot grant a sphere, talent, spell, technique, language, proficiency, or permanent bonus by itself.
### Field and Kitchen Roster - Duty, Household
You understand the quiet backbone of sect life: who cooks, who harvests, who cleans, who carries water, who repairs tools, and who gets blamed when no one planned the work.
When you spend 10 minutes organizing ordinary labor for a farm, kitchen, dormitory, stable, laundry, medicine yard, or storehouse, choose a number of willing ordinary workers up to your Proficiency Bonus + Sect Stewardship key ability modifier.
For the next 4 hours, those workers have Advantage on checks made to perform the assigned ordinary labor safely and efficiently.
This does not magically grow crops, increase yields beyond what good labor could accomplish, or replace the Harvesting and Gathering sphere.
### Worker’s Friend - Etiquette, Household
You know the names of the people most disciples ignore.
Servants, cooks, field hands, porters, gate clerks, stable workers, and outer yard laborers are inclined to treat you as respectful unless you have personally mistreated them.
When you ask ordinary workers for non-secret help, directions, warnings, or introductions, you may spend one Merit Die and add it to the social check.
On a success, the worker also tells you one practical complication that higher-status disciples often miss.
### Quiet Apology Method - Etiquette
You know how to apologize without making the insult larger.
When a non-combat social scene worsens because of a minor etiquette mistake, missed honorific, late arrival, improper form, or accidental slight, you may spend one Merit Die and make a Sect Check.
On a success, reduce the immediate social consequence by one step. An offended person may still be annoyed, but they do not escalate the matter unless they already intended to do so.
This does not undo deliberate insults, crimes, betrayals, public humiliation, or loss of face caused by serious failure.
## Foundation Realm Talents
### Inner Gate Pass - Pavilion, Etiquette
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
Your records and manners are good enough to enter places that outer disciples only hear about.
When you Petition the Hall for access to a restricted but non-secret area, such as an inner archive desk, medicine hall waiting room, furnace schedule, inner lecture balcony, guest courtyard, refined tool room, or supervised scripture shelf, roll your Merit Die twice and use the higher result.
The GM may require Inner Standing, a sponsor, a completed mission, or a written pass for sensitive areas.
This talent does not open forbidden vaults, Secret Inheritances, private residences, sealed formations, treasury rooms, or ancestor halls.
### Contribution Exchange - Ledger, Steward
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You know how to turn merit into lawful access.
During downtime in an institution where you have Standing, spend one Merit Die to request one ordinary exchange:
a Common recipe fragment;
supervised use of a mundane workshop;
a basic map;
common medicinal supplies;
common harvesting permissions;
mundane travel papers;
a letter of introduction;
a minor discount on institution-provided services;
permission to borrow a common tool for one duty.
If the request has a gold cost, reduce the cost by 10 percent per number rolled on the Merit Die, to a maximum reduction of 50 percent. This reduction represents legitimate contribution credit, not bargaining.
The GM may cap, deny, or require a mission for rare resources.
### Mission Team Organizer - Mission, Etiquette
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
When you Accept a Duty or mission that can reasonably involve helpers, spend one Merit Die during preparation to organize a small support team.
Choose one support package:
Porters: carry mundane supplies and reduce travel fatigue from ordinary loads.
Guides: provide Advantage on one navigation or local custom check.
Scribes: preserve records and grant Advantage on one later report or audit check.
Assistants: grant Advantage on one downtime tool check.
Witnesses: provide credible testimony that a duty was attempted or completed.
Junior Disciples: help with safe, non-combat tasks appropriate to their training.
The team avoids combat and danger. If placed in combat, disaster, or lethal hazard, they flee, hide, surrender, or require rescue.
### Public Dao Lecture - Pavilion, Lineage
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You can turn a lesson into usable cultivation structure.
When you attend or organize a public lecture for at least 1 hour, choose a topic: a Path, sphere, Realm principle, sect law, ethical rule, cultivation hazard, historical example, or practical duty.
A number of willing listeners up to your Proficiency Bonus + Sect Stewardship key ability modifier may benefit.
Before each listener’s next Long Rest, they may add one Merit Die to one of the following:
a Daily Cultivation Meditation check related to the topic;
a Study check to understand the topic;
an Insight check to apply the topic socially;
a check to avoid a known cultivation mistake described in the lecture.
A creature can benefit from only one Public Dao Lecture per Long Rest.
This does not grant automatic CXP. It helps the listener make better use of ordinary cultivation and study.
### Hall Apprenticeship - Steward, Pavilion
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
Choose one hall where you have trained: Medicine Hall, Scripture Pavilion, Mission Hall, Storehouse, Kitchen, Spirit Field Office, Forge Office, Formation Maintenance Office, Beast Yard, Guest Hall, or another GM-approved department.
When you work in that hall for at least 1 hour, you may spend one Merit Die and choose one benefit:
gain Advantage on one non-combat check using that hall’s tools or records;
reduce the time for one ordinary hall task by half;
identify the proper superior, clerk, or specialist for a problem;
secure supervised access to common equipment;
learn one ordinary rule, fee, or requirement for using the hall.
You may take this talent multiple times, choosing a different hall each time.
### Steward’s Audit - Ledger, Steward
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You can smell corruption in a clean stack of paper.
After spending 10 minutes inspecting records, stores, work rosters, medicine cabinets, mission reports, or contribution ledgers, make a Sect Check.
On a success, learn whether one of the following is present:
missing supplies;
forged entries;
false mission completion;
hidden favoritism;
unusual waste;
unpaid labor;
duplicate claims;
a person using the wrong authority.
On a success by 5 or more, also learn the most likely place to investigate next.
This talent reveals discrepancies, not the full truth behind them.
### Discipline Mediation - Etiquette, Lineage
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You know how to move a dispute from the courtyard to the arbitration bench.
When a non-combat dispute between sect members, workers, villagers, or guests is about to escalate into punishment, humiliation, expulsion, duel, or public scandal, spend one Merit Die and make a Sect Check.
On a success, the immediate escalation is delayed long enough for one formal hearing, apology, witness statement, elder review, or negotiated settlement.
This does not prove anyone innocent. It creates procedure.
A creature who wants violence for its own sake may ignore this talent, but doing so can create a social consequence if the institution values procedure.
### Courtyard Allotment - Household, Steward
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You can secure a place where people can breathe.
When you spend downtime in an institution where you have at least Outer Standing, you may spend one Merit Die to secure modest private or semi-private space for yourself and a small group for 24 hours.
The space may be a guest room, side courtyard, shed, classroom, empty shrine room, unused lecture alcove, medicine cot, or supervised cultivation room.
While using the space for rest, study, crafting, ordinary healing, or cultivation meditation, one creature may add your Merit Die to one relevant non-combat check.
This talent cannot create luxury, privacy against high-ranking inspection, or a room inside a forbidden area.
### Medicine Hall Queue - Steward, Household
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You know when to wait, when to bow, and which clerk decides whether a minor injury becomes a major problem.
When you Petition the Hall for ordinary medical treatment, antidotes, bandages, supervised recovery, or a Medicine Hall appointment, roll your Merit Die twice and use the higher result.
In addition, during downtime you may spend one Merit Die to reduce by half the wait time for one ordinary treatment, if the institution has capacity.
This does not create pills or replace Alchemy, Life, Blood, or Medicine abilities.
### Stipend Discipline - Ledger
Prerequisite: CL 5+.
You know how to stretch a monthly stipend, store receipts, and avoid the trap of appearing richer than you are.
When you receive legal pay, stipend, mission reward, sect ration, or institutional support, you may spend one Merit Die to gain one of the following benefits:
avoid one minor fee, bribe, late penalty, or bureaucratic deduction;
identify the safest place to store or convert the payment;
gain Advantage on one check to budget supplies for a non-combat journey or duty;
detect whether a payment is short, marked, cursed by paperwork, or politically dangerous.
This cannot multiply money or generate wealth from nothing.
## Core Formation Realm Talents
### Deacon’s Seal - Steward, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You can speak with recognized departmental authority.
When you are in an institution where you have Inner or Core Standing, you may spend one Merit Die to issue a lawful non-combat instruction to lower-Standing members of your department, hall, mission team, or work crew.
Examples include:
close the storehouse until the audit ends;
assign workers to evacuation duty;
require mission reports by sunset;
move students to the lecture courtyard;
open ordinary records for inspection;
preserve evidence;
send a runner to an elder;
suspend a minor privilege pending review.
Targets obey only if the instruction is lawful, plausible, and within your recognized authority. This is not mind control. People may resist, appeal, lie, delay, or exploit unclear orders.
### Department Ledger - Steward, Ledger
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You can manage a hall-scale project.
When you supervise a non-combat downtime project for at least one day, spend one Merit Die and choose one:
reduce the project’s time by 10 percent per number rolled on the Merit Die, maximum 50 percent;
reduce ordinary wastage, spoilage, or loss by the same percentage;
identify the most likely bottleneck;
protect the project from one minor bureaucratic disruption;
create a clean report that grants Advantage on one later petition for funding, permission, or reinforcements.
This cannot reduce the cost of rare named ingredients, Heavenly Materials, Secret Inheritances, land purchases, bribes, or story-required sacrifices unless the GM permits it.
### Inner Archive Index - Pavilion, Ledger
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You do not know every scripture, but you know how they are shelved.
After spending at least 1 hour in a library, Scripture Pavilion, clan archive, mission record vault, or comparable collection, spend one Merit Die and make a Sect Check.
On a success, find one of the following if it exists and is not forbidden to your current access:
a clue to a technique’s teacher or location;
a safe public summary of a restricted topic;
an old map or mission report;
a recipe title and required permission level;
a precedent in sect law;
a list of known hazards for a region;
a lineage note connecting two people, halls, or events.
On a success by 5 or more, you also learn what permission, Standing, mission, sponsor, or price would be needed to reach the next layer of information.
This talent finds indexes and references. It does not grant secret manuals by itself.
### Disciple Register - Lineage, Steward
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You can responsibly manage juniors.
During downtime, you may register a small group of willing juniors, workers, apprentices, or students under your supervision. The group size can be up to twice your Proficiency Bonus.
While they are assigned to safe, non-combat duties, choose one benefit:
their ordinary work counts as one competent assistant for a downtime project;
they preserve records well enough to grant Advantage on one later audit or report;
they maintain a basic facility such as a classroom, field shed, dormitory room, or medicine queue;
one member may carry a message, document, or ordinary object through safe institutional channels.
If you exploit, endanger, abandon, or humiliate the registered group, lose access to this talent in that institution until you repair the relationship.
### Sect Supply Chain - Steward, Mission
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You can move useful things without pretending logistics are magic.
When you have at least one day of preparation and access to a functioning institution, spend one Merit Die to arrange transport, storage, permits, or scheduling for a non-combat supply movement.
Examples include:
medicine to a village;
grain to an outer dormitory;
common tools to a gathering expedition;
blank talisman paper to a classroom;
winter robes to a mountain outpost;
harvested ingredients to an Alchemy hall;
noncombatants away from a dangerous district.
The GM sets travel time, risk, and cost. This talent reduces confusion and ordinary loss, but it does not make bandits vanish, prevent storms, or create guards unless guards already exist and are assigned.
### Hall of Arbitration - Etiquette, Steward
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You know how to make people answer in front of witnesses.
When a dispute concerns property, duty, mission credit, sect law, hall access, injury, insult, debt, teaching rights, or contribution records, spend one Merit Die to convene or request arbitration if the institution has any recognized process.
Until the hearing, involved parties who care about institutional legitimacy suffer social consequences if they escalate the dispute outside procedure.
At the hearing, you may add the Merit Die to one Sect Check made to present evidence, question a witness, cite a precedent, or preserve face for a settlement.
This does not guarantee justice. Corrupt sects can have corrupt arbitration.
### Emergency Muster - Steward, Household
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You can organize people when panic would waste lives.
As an Action outside combat, or during a disaster scene at the GM’s discretion, spend one Merit Die and issue evacuation, shelter, triage, fireline, floodline, or missing-person instructions to a group that can hear or receive your orders.
Make a Sect Check.
On a success, reduce confusion and ordinary casualties in the affected area. The GM chooses the exact effect, such as:
a crowd exits through safe routes;
workers carry water to the right place;
juniors gather at the assigned courtyard;
records or medicine are saved from a burning hall;
villagers avoid a collapsing bridge;
noncombatants hide instead of running into danger.
This talent cannot defeat an enemy or prevent harm that only combat, magic, or sacrifice could stop.
### Courtyard Grant - Household, Lineage
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You secure a stable place in the sect’s body.
You may spend 30 days, appropriate Standing, and GM-approved cost or patronage to establish a named courtyard, classroom, field office, dormitory wing, guest suite, workroom, or small hall.
While using this place for non-combat work, you and your invited allies gain the following benefits:
regain all Merit Dice after a Long Rest if you perform 10 minutes of duties there;
gain Advantage on one Sect Check per day made inside the place;
safely store ordinary records, tools, clothing, and non-rare supplies;
maintain a small staff or student group if you have Disciple Register.
If the place is attacked, seized, politically undermined, or neglected, the benefits pause until restored.
### Public Merit Roll - Ledger, Etiquette
Prerequisite: CL 10+.
You can make service visible.
After a mission, project, disaster response, harvest, investigation, teaching period, or public duty, spend one Merit Die and make a Sect Check to prepare a fair public account.
On a success, choose one:
one overlooked worker receives proper credit;
one false claimant is exposed as suspicious;
one ally gains temporary improved Standing for one scene;
one rival cannot easily erase your contribution;
one superior receives a clean summary and is less likely to misunderstand the result.
On a success by 5 or more, choose two.
This talent does not force corrupt superiors to act fairly, but it makes unfairness more visible.
## Nascent Soul Realm Talents
### Elder’s Mandate - Lineage, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
Your words can become institution-level instruction.
When you have Elder Standing, equivalent authority, or a recognized emergency commission, spend one Merit Die to issue a non-combat mandate affecting one hall, branch, expedition, village protectorate, archive, medicine ward, or comparable department.
Examples include:
open emergency grain stores;
suspend a dangerous mission board category;
require a public accounting of missing disciples;
grant temporary guest protection to refugees;
authorize a medical quarantine;
assign a branch hall to prepare for winter;
order the archive to preserve threatened records;
call all juniors of a lineage to public instruction.
The mandate lasts until fulfilled, revoked, or challenged by equal or higher authority.
This cannot order attacks, assassinations, forced cultivation, theft, executions, or other combat actions through the sphere.
### Found a Branch Hall - Lineage, Steward, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
You can plant a sect presence without mistaking a building for a lineage.
With GM approval, appropriate authority, 1 year of work, and major resources, you may found or reorganize a branch hall, village protectorate, outer academy, medicine station, scripture school, pilgrimage lodge, field office, or resource outpost.
Choose the branch’s first purpose:
teaching;
medicine;
farming administration;
gathering permissions;
archive preservation;
mission staging;
guest diplomacy;
disaster relief;
trade oversight;
outer disciple training.
While present in the branch and acting according to its purpose, your Merit Die increases by one step, to a maximum of d10.
The branch is a strategic asset. It can be attacked, corrupted, starved, praised, taxed, abandoned, or inherited.
### Ancestral Writ - Pavilion, Lineage, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
You can invoke the old words correctly.
When you have access to a legitimate ancestral record, founding oath, old charter, saint’s instruction, sect law, clan genealogy, or lineage tablet, spend one Merit Die and make a Sect Check.
On a success, the Writ grants one non-combat effect:
delay an unlawful punishment until review;
open a sealed but non-secret archive category;
protect a junior from being erased from records;
require a hall to answer a formal question;
preserve a property, title, or duty from casual seizure;
grant safe guest status to a named petitioner for 24 hours;
prove that a tradition, promise, or boundary once existed.
On a failure, the Writ is ambiguous, politically inconvenient, incomplete, or challenged.
This talent cannot compel an ancestor to appear, create a divine miracle, or override a current sect master without consequence.
### Ten Thousand Disciples’ Ledger - Ledger, Steward, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
You can read a sect as a living circulation system.
After spending at least one day reviewing reports, rosters, ledgers, petitions, stores, and mission outcomes for a large institution, spend one Merit Die and make a Sect Check.
On a success, learn the answer to three of the following:
Which hall is under-resourced?
Which duty is creating resentment?
Which mission category is consuming too many disciples?
Which storehouse is being quietly drained?
Which elder’s policy is failing in practice?
Which group is about to revolt, desert, or collapse?
Which ordinary workers are holding the sect together?
Which resource shortage will become a crisis next season?
Which branch is lying in its reports?
Which junior or clerk deserves promotion?
On a success by 5 or more, also identify one practical reform that would help without requiring combat.
This talent gives administrative truth, not automatic political power.
### Secret Realm Charter - Mission, Steward, Lineage
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
You can prepare an expedition as an institution, not a mob.
When a sect, clan, academy, or alliance prepares a secret realm, ruin, inheritance ground, dangerous harvest, diplomatic mission, disaster survey, or pilgrimage, spend one Merit Die during planning and make a Sect Check.
On a success, create a Charter that defines:
who is responsible for the expedition;
who may enter;
who records discoveries;
how gathered resources are divided;
how injuries and deaths are reported;
what must not be touched;
who speaks for the institution outside the gate.
While the Charter is respected, participants have Advantage on non-combat checks made to resolve disputes about access, shares, reports, and duties.
This talent cannot make the secret realm safer by itself and does not grant combat bonuses inside it.
### Open the Grand Archive - Pavilion, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
Once per campaign arc, or once per year of downtime with GM approval, you may spend one Merit Die to petition for access to a Grand Archive, ancestral catalogue, restricted scripture index, founding record, sealed court transcript, or comparable high institution record.
Make a Sect Check.
On a success, you do not gain a secret technique directly. Instead, choose one:
learn the true name of a lost scripture;
learn the required trial for a Secret Inheritance;
discover which elder or branch controls a forbidden record;
identify a historical lie in the sect’s public story;
find the map to a lost branch hall, medicine garden, battlefield, tomb, or oath site;
learn why a technique was banned, sealed, or restricted.
On a failure, you learn what price, sponsor, trial, rank, or political concession would be needed to try again.
### Preserve the Lineage - Lineage, Household, Realm Evolution
Prerequisite: CL 15+.
When a sect, branch, hall, village protectorate, archive, disciple cohort, or lineage household faces collapse from disaster, scandal, famine, evacuation, invasion aftermath, plague, corruption, or succession crisis, you may spend one Merit Die and commit yourself to preservation.
For the next 30 days, while you personally work on non-combat preservation, you may add your Merit Die to one relevant check per day without spending it.
Valid checks include:
evacuation;
rationing;
archive preservation;
medical triage;
witness collection;
debt negotiation;
inheritance transfer;
morale recovery;
refugee placement;
junior disciple protection;
lawful succession;
rebuilding a hall.
At the end of the 30 days, if the institution survives in some recognizable form, regain all spent Merit Dice and gain one lasting institutional relationship chosen with the GM.
This talent does not guarantee victory against enemies. It gives structure to saving what remains.
## Secret Inheritance
### Name on the Ancestral Ledger
Prerequisite: CL 17+, Sect Stewardship, Found a Branch Hall or Ancestral Writ, GM approval.
Your name is not merely written in the register. The register answers when your duty is called.
Once per campaign arc, when you stand before a legitimate institutional witness such as an ancestral tablet, sect master, elder council, founding stele, branch altar, public merit roll, grand archive, or oath hall, you may invoke your recorded service.
State one non-combat institutional claim:
this junior is under my protection;
this village is owed shelter;
this archive must not be burned;
this mission’s dead must be named;
this medicine must be distributed;
this branch hall must be heard;
this false charge must face witnesses;
this oath has not expired;
this person deserves a place in the register;
this sect must remember what it promised.
Make a Sect Check. Add all unspent Merit Dice you possess to the roll, expending them.
On a success, the institution must answer the claim in a meaningful way before it can lawfully ignore, punish, erase, or overrule it. The answer may be help, hearing, delay, protection, recognition, public debate, a trial, or a demand for repayment.
On a failure, the claim is heard but contested. You learn who or what stands against it.
This inheritance does not compel cosmic justice, defeat tyrants, or rewrite the sect’s heart. It ensures that service has a voice strong enough to force the powerful to speak back.
## Variant Scriptures
A Sect Stewardship Variant Scripture narrows the way you can gain and spend Merit Dice in exchange for a stronger identity.
Unless the GM says otherwise:
choose at most one Sect Stewardship Variant Scripture when first learning the sphere or undergoing a major institutional initiation;
Variant Scriptures are mutually exclusive unless the GM permits otherwise;
the limitation applies to all Sect Stewardship talents you use;
if a Variant Scripture grants a talent whose prerequisites you do not yet meet, you gain that talent when you meet the prerequisites;
if the Variant Scripture is removed, retrained, or rejected, lose the bonus talent or benefit it granted.
### Outer Gate Drudge Scripture
“The lowest broom hears the most secrets.”
Limitation: Your Merit Dice can be regained only through ordinary labor, service, cleaning, farming, kitchen work, carrying, repair, dormitory duty, messenger work, or comparable low-status duties. You have Disadvantage on Sect Stewardship checks to command, supervise, or claim high-status privileges unless you have earned temporary authority in the scene.
Benefit: Gain Outer Gate Routine as a bonus talent. When you complete an ordinary labor Duty, you may also learn one practical rumor from workers, servants, or outer disciples involved.
### Inner Gate Scholar Scripture
“A correct note today becomes a door tomorrow.”
Limitation: You regain Merit Dice only by attending lectures, copying texts, organizing records, maintaining archives, studying rules, or helping with instruction. You cannot spend Merit Dice on petitions for food, lodging, tools, or labor unless the request directly supports study, teaching, or recordkeeping.
Benefit: Gain Common Scripture Notes as a bonus talent. At CL 5, gain Public Dao Lecture as a bonus talent when you meet its prerequisites.
### Mission Hall Runner Scripture
“The board is a map of hunger, ambition, and unpaid debts.”
Limitation: You regain Merit Dice only by accepting, completing, filing, witnessing, or preparing missions and duties. You cannot spend Merit Dice on petitions unrelated to a current mission, writ, or assigned task.
Benefit: Gain Notice Board Reader as a bonus talent. When you Accept a Duty from a mission board, gain one temporary Merit Die for that Duty. This temporary die vanishes when the Duty ends.
### Sect Servant Scripture
“A servant sees the sect from beneath its robes.”
Limitation: Your Standing cannot rise above Servant or Outer through this sphere alone. You have Disadvantage on Sect Stewardship checks that rely on formal rank over free disciples, but Advantage on checks involving servants, kitchens, laundry, menial labor, hidden passages of service, and overlooked practical needs.
Benefit: Gain Worker’s Friend as a bonus talent. When you Petition the Hall for survival needs or worker help, treat a Merit Die result of 1 as a 4.
### Clan Steward Scripture
“Blood remembers what paper denies. Paper remembers what blood forgets.”
Limitation: Your Sect Stewardship features work only within a clan, family estate, sworn household, village lineage, noble branch, or institution where kinship and household obligation matter. You cannot use Merit Dice to gain access to cultivation sect facilities unless your clan has recognized ties there.
Benefit: Gain Clean Ledger Hand as a bonus talent. When resolving inheritance, marriage alliance, household property, kinship duty, funeral duty, or clan seniority, increase your Merit Die by one step, maximum d10.
### Guest Official Scripture
“A guest has fewer roots, but cleaner hands.”
Limitation: You do not regain Merit Dice from ordinary internal chores unless you are formally invited to perform them. Your Standing in any host institution begins as Guest Standing and may be revoked if you violate guest rites.
Benefit: Gain Sect Token Etiquette as a bonus talent. When you enter a new institution peacefully and present yourself honestly, you may spend one Merit Die to secure a formal audience with an appropriate clerk, steward, guest master, or outer deacon if such a person is available.
## Path Synergies
### Body Refining - Labor Tempering
A Body Refiner may express Sect Stewardship through work that trains the body: hauling water, carrying stone, repairing roofs, plowing fields, cleaning furnace pits, maintaining training yards, and protecting weaker workers from exhaustion.
When your chosen Sect Role involves physical labor, you may use Constitution as your Sect Stewardship key ability for checks tied to that role.
Once per Long Rest, when you regain Merit Dice through physical labor, regain Stamina equal to your Proficiency Bonus. This cannot exceed your maximum Stamina and cannot be used during combat.
### Qi Cultivation - Ledger of Circulation
A Qi Cultivator may express Sect Stewardship through schedules, resource flow, spirit-stone accounting, lecture cycles, hall ventilation, furnace slots, spirit field rotations, and mission timing.
When you complete a Duty that improves a sect’s cultivation infrastructure, such as preparing a meditation hall, arranging a lecture, preserving a spirit field schedule, or organizing furnace access, you may regain CP equal to half your Proficiency Bonus, rounded down, minimum 1.
You can regain CP this way once per Long Rest and never during combat.
### Spirit Awakening - Lineage Listening
A Spirit Awakener may express Sect Stewardship through names, oaths, grief, teaching bonds, inherited shame, neglected servants, old promises, and the emotional truth behind duty.
When you use Dormitory Network, Discipline Mediation, Public Merit Roll, Ancestral Writ, or Preserve the Lineage, you may use Wisdom as your Sect Stewardship key ability.
Once per Long Rest, when you complete a Duty that repairs trust, protects a junior, preserves a name, or honors the dead, gain Advantage on your next saving throw against a Heart Demon, fear, charm, possession, or spiritual despair effect before your next Long Rest.
## Cultivation Insights
### Proper Seniority
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, CL 4+.
You instinctively know how to address seniors, peers, juniors, guests, servants, deacons, elders, and strangers without causing unnecessary offense.
Gain proficiency in Insight or Persuasion. If you already have proficiency, gain a flat +2 bonus to checks with the chosen skill when navigating hierarchy, etiquette, or institutional duty.
Once per Long Rest, when a social scene would worsen because you used the wrong title, gesture, bow, seating order, or speaking order, you may reroll the check that caused the mistake.
### Ledger-Bound Memory
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, CL 4+.
You keep records that other people can actually use.
You always know where your own mission writs, receipts, duty notes, petitions, and contribution records are unless they are stolen, destroyed, or sealed by a stronger effect.
When you make a Sect Check involving records you personally maintained, roll one Merit Die and add it without expending it. You can use this benefit once per Long Rest.
### Outer Gate Beloved
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, CL 4+.
You are known for treating low-status people as people.
Servants, workers, outer disciples, farmers, cooks, porters, and clerks who have heard of you begin one step friendlier unless you or your allies have harmed them.
When you Petition the Hall through low-status channels, you may include one extra creature per point of your Proficiency Bonus.
### Mission-Born Reputation
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, Notice Board Reader, CL 8+.
Your completed missions speak before you do.
When you complete a mission or Duty with witnesses and file a clean report, choose one institution involved. For the next 30 days, you have Advantage on one Sect Check there when requesting a similar mission, asking for related information, or defending the truth of your report.
You may maintain a number of active Mission-Born Reputations equal to your Proficiency Bonus.
### Hall Insider
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, Hall Apprenticeship, CL 8+.
Choose one hall for which you have Hall Apprenticeship.
Your Merit Die increases by one step, maximum d8, for Sect Stewardship checks made inside that hall or directly involving its procedures.
When you spend one Merit Die to Petition that hall, treat a result of 1 as a 3.
You may take this Insight multiple times, choosing a different hall each time.
### Deacon’s Patience
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, CL 12+.
You have learned that most crises become worse when the first person with authority panics.
When you fail a Sect Check related to mediation, emergency organization, audit, records, logistics, or duty assignment, you may spend one Merit Die after seeing the result and add it to the roll, even if you already spent a Merit Die on that check.
You can use this Insight once per Long Rest.
### Branch Founder
Prerequisite: Sect Stewardship, Courtyard Grant or Department Ledger, CL 12+.
When you establish a courtyard, classroom, workroom, small hall, field office, or branch project, reduce the ordinary time required by 25 percent.
In addition, your project begins with one loyal mundane assistant, clerk, worker, student, or caretaker chosen with the GM. This assistant is not a combat companion and will not enter dangerous scenes unless specifically protected.

