spheres.full_exact_source_text:
# Sphere of Spirit Companions
<small><em>Qi Expression: Primary — Companion Spirit Qi; Supporting — Bond Qi, Beast/Ancestral Qi, Soul Qi, Oath Qi.</em></small>
"A spirit is not a beast wearing a ghost's skin. A spirit is a living answer to a place, grief, hunger, oath, element, memory, corpse, star, song, tree, blade, shrine, or sin. To bind one is to touch a Dao that was already walking before you arrived."
Common Name: Spirit Companions Possible Catalogue Name: Sphere of Spirit Companions
Sphere Type: Companion / Pact / Spirit-Bond Sphere
Primary Path: Spirit Awakening
Secondary Path: Qi Cultivation Body Refining Access: Legal through hosted spirits, body-bound spirits, bloodline spirits, weapon spirits, parasite spirits, oath scars, inner shrines, and spirits that act as their own external source. A Body Refiner does not personally project external qi through this sphere unless a feature explicitly permits it.
Resource: CP, spirit action economy, offerings, Bond Strain, and Spirit Nature permissions
Core Chassis: Spirit Nature, Spirit Bonds, Manifestation, Bond Strain, Spirit Nature Expressions
Core Abilities: Read the Spirit Nature, Forge the Bond, Manifest the Spirit Nature
## Design Intent
This sphere replaces the old broad "Spirit" concept with a focused rule system for spirit relationships.
The old problem is that "spirit" can mean too many things at once: ghost, familiar, elemental, ancestor, demon, dryad, weapon soul, shrine guardian, possession, animal spirit, local god, bound servant, pact patron, or inner parasite. This sphere narrows that into one playable chassis:
A cultivator interacts with a spirit, defines the spirit's Nature, creates or exploits a Bond, and then allows that spirit's Nature to manifest in play.
The important rule is this:
A bound spirit is not just a trained animal. It has its own Nature. That Nature determines what sphere-like effects it can express.
Beastmastery handles animals, packs, mounts, monsters, and living beasts. Spirit Companions handles spirits with personhood, alien impulse, sacred or profane Nature, contract logic, hosting, binding, and sphere expression through the spirit's own essence.
## Dao of Spirit Companions
Spirit Companions cultivates relationship with powers that already have meaning.
A beast can be trained. A spirit must be acknowledged, bargained with, hosted, worshiped, contained, deceived, fed, redeemed, broken, or befriended.
The Dao of this sphere is not "control." Control is only one method, and often the worst one.
The Dao of this sphere asks:
What is the spirit, what does it want, what does it answer to, and what are you willing to become by sharing its nature?
A Spirit Companion cultivator may be:
- a respectful medium who speaks to local spirits;
- a shrine disciple who carries ancestral guardians;
- a wandering exorcist who frees bound spirits;
- a battlefield summoner who fights beside oath-bound spirits;
- a Body Refiner who hosts a blood parasite or weapon spirit;
- a Qi Cultivator who negotiates with elemental servants;
- a Spirit Awakener who shares dreams, wounds, and identity with a soul companion;
- a wicked binder who chains spirits against their will;
- a half-possessed vessel wrestling with the being inside their bones.
## What This Sphere Is For
Use Spirit Companions when a character's power comes from one or more spirits as characters, allies, inhabitants, prisoners, patrons, or co-cultivators.
This sphere supports three major modes.
Spirit Companionship. A willing spirit travels with you, fights with you, advises you, lends you its Nature, and grows through relationship.
Spirit Binding. A spirit is sealed, constrained, imprisoned, compelled, or weaponized. This is possible, often effective, and often immoral. The sphere supports it because cultivation stories include it, not because the text assumes it is righteous.
Spirit Hosting. A spirit dwells in your body, soul, weapon, shadow, bloodline, dantian, bones, inner palace, or dreams. It may lend power, speak through you, protect you, tempt you, transform you, or try to replace you.
## Sphere Boundaries
Spirit Companions overlaps many spheres, but does not replace them.
Beastmastery. Beastmastery trains, bonds, rides, commands, and evolves beasts and living companions. Spirit Companions handles spirits with Spirit Nature, pact logic, manifestation, hosting, and sphere expression. A spirit beast may use either sphere depending on whether it is primarily beast or spirit.
Death. Death governs corpses, ghosts, graves, funerary authority, necromantic endings, and the dead as dead. Spirit Companions can form a relationship with a ghost or ancestor, but does not automatically command death energy, animate corpses, or create undead.
Daemonic Cultivation. Daemonic Cultivation handles demonic corruption, contracts, predation, monstrous transformation, and forbidden bargains with daemonic powers. Spirit Companions can bind or host a demon-like spirit, but the daemonic corruption itself belongs to Daemonic Cultivation.
Puppetry. Puppetry controls bodies, dolls, strings, and puppet-vessels. Spirit Companions may allow a spirit to inhabit a vessel, but it does not grant puppet armies or body-control techniques by default.
Karma. Karma governs debt, consequence, vow, fate-weight, and moral entanglement. Spirit Companions may create oaths and obligations, but does not automatically rewrite karmic debt.
The Hidden Eye. The Hidden Eye sees hidden truths, omens, and secret paths. Spirit Companions can ask spirits what they know, but does not automatically grant divination.
Dreams. Dreams governs sleep, dreamwalking, nightmare, memory-dream, and inner vision. Spirit Companions can include dream spirits or hosted dream companions, but dream realms and dream attacks remain Dreams sphere territory.
Life. Life restores living patterns. A healing spirit may use Life as part of its Nature, but this sphere does not itself provide healing unless the spirit's Nature or a talent grants it.
Flowers / Verdant Growth. A dryad, flower spirit, wood spirit, or jungle spirit may express Flowers or Verdant Growth as part of its Nature. The cultivator is not automatically a plant cultivator; the spirit is the source.
Formations. Formations creates arrays, formation fields, and environmental inscriptions. Spirit Companions can anchor a shrine or spirit residence, but does not replace formation craft.
Sect Stewardship. Sect Stewardship manages institutions, disciples, facilities, and social structures. Spirit Companions may negotiate with local guardian spirits, but does not govern the sect as an organization.
## Rules Baseline
This draft assumes the current Tianxia player-facing sphere compendium and the v2.5 canon sphere-list override.
A spirit's Nature may reference legal spheres from the current canon list. If the spirit's Nature references a sphere that has not been rebuilt or approved, the GM should either replace it with a legal equivalent or treat it as special NPC-only material.
This sphere does not automatically grant full access to any other sphere. It creates permission for the spirit to express its own Nature. Whether that expression uses a full sphere talent, a simplified Nature Expression, or a stat-block action depends on the spirit's design and the character's investment.
## Relationship to Chapter 13: Bonded Companions
This sphere does not replace the companion rules in Chapter 13: Bonded Companions of Cultivation: The Immortal's Path.
Chapter 13 remains the rule source for full combat-capable bonded companions: companion archetypes, companion statistics, companion attacks, command action economy, Realm Evolutions, restoration after death, and the normal limit on full bonded companions.
Spirit Companions handles something different: relationships with spirits, spirit Natures, offerings, taboos, hosted presences, binding seals, and the way a spirit's own Nature can express sphere-like effects.
A spirit bond from this sphere is not automatically a Chapter 13 bonded companion.
A spirit does not gain Chapter 13 companion statistics, companion attacks, automatic Realm Evolutions, companion restoration rituals, or full independent combat presence unless a separate source explicitly upgrades it into a full combat-capable bonded companion.
Such a source might be:
- a Cultivation Insight;
- a major roleplaying reward;
- a specific sphere talent that says the spirit becomes a full bonded companion;
- a sect inheritance;
- a forged technique;
- an artifact, shrine, bloodline, or GM-approved story benefit.
Most spirits created or contacted through this sphere are one of the following instead:
- a Minor Spirit Agreement that provides advice, omens, safe passage, or small favors;
- a Major Spirit Bond that matters mechanically but does not have a full companion stat block;
- a Hosted Spirit that lends a body-bound Nature Expression;
- a Bound Spirit held in a seal, weapon, shrine, or vessel;
- a Manifesting Spirit Source that briefly produces a Nature Expression, then withdraws;
- a full Chapter 13 bonded companion only if separately granted.
If a character has a normal Chapter 13 bonded companion and also has Minor Spirit Agreements or hosted spirits from this sphere, those lesser spirit relationships do not count against the Chapter 13 one-companion limit unless they become full combat-capable bonded companions.
If a spirit is upgraded into a full Chapter 13 bonded companion, use Chapter 13 for its body, durability, command economy, attacks, Realm Evolutions, and death/restoration rules. Use this sphere only for its Spirit Nature, offerings, taboos, bond strain, hosting, binding ethics, and Nature-sphere expression.
## Key Terms
### Spirit
A spirit is a non-ordinary being whose existence is defined by Nature rather than biology alone.
Examples include:
- ancestor shades;
- local gods;
- shrine spirits;
- river spirits;
- dryads;
- fox spirits;
- sword spirits;
- beast spirits;
- hungry ghosts;
- demons;
- plague spirits;
- storm spirits;
- dream companions;
- blood parasites;
- guardian spirits;
- corpse-lamps;
- memory spirits;
- inner devils;
- weapon souls.
A spirit may have a body, but its body is not the whole truth of it.
### Spirit Nature
A spirit's Nature is the set of concepts, hungers, spheres, taboos, memories, and authorities that define what it can do.
A Spirit Nature should include:
- Nature Name: Blood Dryad, Ancestor-General, River Child, Hungry Bronze Mask, Mirror Fox, Ashen Funeral Moth, etc.
- Nature
Tags: short descriptors such as Blood, Flowers, Ambush, Song, Parasite, Shrine, Guardian, Poison, Dream, Blade, Weather.
- Nature Spheres: one to three legal spheres the spirit may express through talents, stat-block actions, or Nature Expressions.
- Temperament: loyal, proud, hungry, playful, bitter, sacred, alien, predatory, timid, scholarly, wrathful.
- Need / Offering: what sustains the bond.
- Taboo: what offends, weakens, or risks rebellion.
- Manifestation: how it appears or acts when called.
A simple spirit may have one Nature Sphere. A complex spirit may have two or three. Great spirits, ancient spirits, and major NPC spirits may have more with GM permission.
### Nature Sphere
A Nature Sphere is a legal sphere that belongs to the spirit's Nature.
If a blood dryad has Flowers and Blood as Nature Spheres, that means the spirit can potentially manifest flower-fields, blood-vines, predatory bloom, or blood-drinking roots through the bond.
The cultivator does not automatically learn Flowers or Blood by bonding that spirit.
### Spirit Nature Expression
A Spirit Nature Expression is a controlled way for the spirit to use its Nature during play.
A Nature Expression may be:
- a simplified effect written in the spirit's stat block;
- a base ability from one of the spirit's Nature Spheres;
- a talent from one of the spirit's Nature Spheres;
- a forged spirit technique;
- a Secret Inheritance expression;
- an NPC-only ability approved by the GM.
When a PC uses this sphere, Spirit Nature Expressions should be purchased, unlocked, or explicitly granted. The spirit should not become a free second full cultivator unless the GM intentionally wants that.
### Spirit Bond
A Spirit Bond is the active connection between the cultivator and the spirit.
There are three default Bond Modes:
- Covenant Bond: willing companionship.
- Binding Seal: coercive or restrictive binding.
- Hosting Bond: the spirit dwells in or upon the cultivator, an item, a body, a shadow, a shrine, or another vessel.
A spirit may have more than one Bond Mode if the story supports it. For example, a willing weapon spirit may be both a Covenant companion and Hosted in a spear.
### Bond Strain
Bond Strain measures stress, disagreement, overuse, spiritual backlash, coercion, or instability in the bond.
Bond Strain ranges from 0 to 3.
At 0 Strain, the bond is stable.
At 1 Strain, the spirit becomes irritated, hungry, restless, or more difficult to call.
At 2 Strain, the bond becomes dangerous. The spirit may demand an offering, impose a roleplaying complication, lose access to one expression for a scene, or create a visible sign of instability.
At 3 Strain, the bond reaches crisis. The GM chooses or rolls an appropriate crisis:
- the spirit refuses a command;
- the spirit acts according to its Nature instead of your intent;
- the spirit retreats into its vessel;
- the spirit manifests uncontrolled;
- the spirit lashes out at an enemy and an ally nearby;
- a Binding Seal weakens;
- a Hosted spirit contests control;
- the spirit demands an immediate offering;
- a taboo consequence occurs.
After a crisis resolves, reduce Bond Strain to 1 unless the story says otherwise.
### Offering
An Offering is something given to the spirit to sustain, honor, calm, strengthen, or manipulate the bond.
Offerings may be material, emotional, spiritual, behavioral, or narrative.
Examples include:
- incense;
- blood;
- song;
- dance;
- milk;
- flowers;
- rare metals;
- burial rites;
- memories;
- secrets;
- victory;
- fresh prey;
- honest praise;
- a vow kept;
- a taboo avoided;
- a shrine repaired;
- a corpse avenged.
The GM decides whether an offering is sufficient for a specific spirit.
### Taboo
A Taboo is something the spirit cannot tolerate.
Violating a Taboo usually increases Bond Strain by 1 or more.
Examples include:
- breaking a vow;
- entering a polluted shrine;
- eating a forbidden food;
- sparing a hated enemy;
- killing a protected creature;
- lying to the spirit;
- denying burial rites;
- refusing blood;
- mocking the spirit's old name;
- using the spirit against its home;
- forcing manifestation during a sacred day;
- commanding a bound spirit to violate its core Nature.
## Body Refiner Access Rule
This sphere is important for Body Refiners because it creates a clean separation between the Body Refiner's own internal cultivation and the spirit's own external Nature.
A Body Refiner using Spirit Companions may:
- host a spirit inside the body, blood, bones, dantian, weapon, skin, shadow, scars, or inner shrine;
- let a manifest spirit use its own Nature externally;
- coordinate a companion spirit's Nature Expression;
- use a hosted spirit's power only through self, touch, held weapons, natural weapons, blood contact, body aura, or forged body-bound techniques unless a talent says otherwise;
- bind a spirit into a weapon, armor, chain, tattoo, mask, or bloodline scar.
A Body Refiner may not personally project external qi through this sphere simply because the spirit has an external Nature.
If the spirit manifests and casts a flower field, storm-call, curse, dream song, or similar effect, the source is the spirit, not the Body Refiner. The character sheet should say this clearly.
## Spirit Nature Action Economy
This section governs spirit Nature Expressions, not the ordinary action economy of a Chapter 13 bonded companion.
If a spirit has been upgraded into a full Chapter 13 bonded companion, use Chapter 13 for its movement, Dodge default, attacks, reactions, command rules, and Realm Evolutions. Then use this section only when the spirit expresses its special Spirit Nature.
If the spirit is not a full Chapter 13 bonded companion, it does not automatically take turns, make attacks, or function as a second creature in combat. It acts through brief manifestations, hosted effects, omens, shrine flares, vessel movements, or Nature Expressions granted by this sphere.
Unless a talent, stat block, or GM ruling says otherwise:
- a harmless manifestation, advice, omen, emotional impression, or sensory flare requires no action or occurs as part of the relevant scene;
- a simple Nature Expression usually requires your Bonus Action and any listed CP or Offering cost;
- a stronger Nature Expression equivalent to a sphere ability, area effect, condition, attack, or major battlefield change usually requires your Action;
- if the spirit has its own full stat block and independent CP pool, the GM may let it act independently, but it should count as a major ally for encounter design.
A spirit without a full companion stat block cannot make ordinary companion attacks unless a specific talent, Insight, roleplaying reward, or GM-created stat block grants that ability.
## Spirit CP and Costs
When a spirit uses a Nature Expression through your Spirit Companion sphere, use one of the following cost models.
Default PC Model: You pay the CP cost. This prevents the spirit from becoming a free second cultivator.
Spirit Stat Block Model: The spirit has its own CP or limited-use abilities. The spirit pays its own cost. This is appropriate for NPC allies, major bound spirits, or special campaign characters.
Shared Cost Model: You and the spirit each pay part of the cost, or you pay CP while the spirit pays Offering, Bond Strain, or manifestation risk.
Unless the GM says otherwise, player characters use the Default PC Model.
## Spirit Bond Limit
You may maintain one Major Spirit Bond at a time.
A Major Spirit Bond is a bound spirit, hosted spirit, weapon spirit, ancestor guide, shrine guardian, covenant spirit, or similar spirit whose abilities matter in play.
This is a spirit relationship limit, not the same rule as the Chapter 13 limit on full combat-capable bonded companions.
At Core Formation Realm, you may maintain two Major Spirit Bonds.
At Nascent Soul Realm, you may maintain three Major Spirit Bonds.
You may maintain a number of Minor Spirit Agreements equal to your Proficiency Bonus. A Minor Spirit Agreement can provide advice, flavor, a small omen, safe passage through its home, or other noncombat utility. It cannot fight for you, use combat actions, or create major sphere effects unless upgraded to a Major Spirit Bond.
A Major Spirit Bond still does not become a full Chapter 13 bonded companion unless a separate source explicitly says so.
## Spirit Relationship Chassis
A spirit created, contacted, hosted, or bound through this sphere should first be written as a relationship and Nature, not as a full companion stat block.
Every significant spirit should have:
- a Spirit Nature;
- one or more Nature Tags;
- one or more Nature Spheres or simplified Nature Expressions;
- one Bond Mode;
- one Need / Offering;
- one Taboo;
- a manifestation description;
- a clear statement of whether it is a Minor Spirit Agreement, Major Spirit Bond, Hosted Spirit, Bound Spirit, Manifesting Spirit Source, or full Chapter 13 bonded companion.
Only spirits that are separately upgraded into full combat-capable companions need Chapter 13 companion archetypes, companion attacks, companion hit points, Realm Evolutions, and restoration rules.
When a spirit needs a combat body and no specific stat block exists, the GM may choose one of three models:
Nature Expression Only. The spirit has no combat stat block. It appears through this sphere's abilities and Nature Expressions.
Simple Spirit Stat Block. The GM creates a limited stat block for a scene, encounter, or special technique. It does not automatically gain Chapter 13 evolutions.
Chapter 13 Bonded Companion. The spirit is a full combat-capable bonded companion gained through an Insight, roleplaying reward, specific talent, inheritance, or GM approval. Use Chapter 13 for its combat body and this sphere for its Nature.
A spirit should not receive a full independent character sheet unless the GM wants that spirit to be a major NPC.
